WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.090
Day 19 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.404
Cost as a quality metric: top-K prefiltering before the model.

00:00:08.954 --> 00:00:13.512
How to cut an agent's input tokens with a local candidate prefilter and prove with

00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:15.679
measurements that quality doesn't drop.

00:00:16.979 --> 00:00:20.093
In QA we're used to quality being measured in defects.

00:00:20.393 --> 00:00:25.134
With systems that use models, cost becomes an equally concrete quality dimension: a

00:00:25.134 --> 00:00:29.474
feature that gets it right but costs too much per call can't be sustained in

00:00:29.474 --> 00:00:32.958
production, and one that's cheap and fails is no good either.

00:00:33.258 --> 00:00:35.408
Someone has to measure both together.

00:00:36.708 --> 00:00:41.224
The most common case of unnecessary cost is sending the model all the available

00:00:41.224 --> 00:00:42.481
context, just in case.

00:00:42.781 --> 00:00:47.403
This guide shows a technique to avoid it: a local prefilter that picks the K most

00:00:47.403 --> 00:00:49.970
relevant candidates before calling the model.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:54.952
And it shows how to test it, because a badly measured prefilter lowers cost and

00:00:54.952 --> 00:00:57.204
quality alike without anyone noticing.

00:00:58.504 --> 00:01:00.202
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:00.752 --> 00:01:04.551
The day 18 case: names to map against a catalog.

00:01:05.101 --> 00:01:07.902
A labeled set with the correct answer per name.

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.481
This is the setup.

00:01:10.781 --> 00:01:14.998
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:16.298 --> 00:01:17.334
Step 1.

00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:18.647
The baseline.

00:01:19.197 --> 00:01:24.000
Measure the baseline: input tokens per call and how much is fixed context (for

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.725
example, the whole catalog).

00:01:26.275 --> 00:01:28.309
Before optimizing, measure.

00:01:28.609 --> 00:01:33.299
For a sample of calls, record how many input tokens each one uses and how many

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:38.229
correspond to fixed context repeated every time, such as a whole catalog or a full

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:38.770
document.

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:42.242
In many systems that fixed part is most of the cost.

00:01:42.542 --> 00:01:45.958
Also note current quality with the eval you already have.

00:01:46.258 --> 00:01:50.808
Without those two numbers, tokens and quality, you won't be able to say afterwards

00:01:50.808 --> 00:01:53.970
whether the change was an improvement or merely a change.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.469
Step 2.

00:01:56.769 --> 00:01:58.687
A simple local prefilter.

00:01:59.237 --> 00:02:02.931
Build a local prefilter that picks the K most similar candidates.

00:02:03.231 --> 00:02:07.193
Start with a simple lexical one: it's free and calls no model.

00:02:07.743 --> 00:02:12.426
The prefilter takes the input and the full candidate list, and returns the K most

00:02:12.426 --> 00:02:12.889
similar.

00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:18.059
Start with a lexical method, for example character trigram similarity: it takes a

00:02:18.059 --> 00:02:21.667
few lines to write, runs in milliseconds and calls no model.

00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:26.393
It's also deterministic, so it can be tested with classic tests.

00:02:26.693 --> 00:02:30.120
The temptation is to jump straight to something more sophisticated.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.138
Resist it until you have a number showing the simple approach falls short.

00:02:35.438 --> 00:02:36.521
Step 3.

00:02:36.821 --> 00:02:38.147
Prefilter recall.

00:02:38.697 --> 00:02:43.790
Measure prefilter recall: in how many labeled cases the correct answer is among the

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:43.912
K.

00:02:44.212 --> 00:02:47.280
If it isn't there, the model can't get it right.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.504
This is the central measurement.

00:02:49.804 --> 00:02:54.427
For each labeled case, check whether the correct answer is among the K candidates

00:02:54.427 --> 00:02:55.740
the prefilter returned.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.189
The percentage of cases where it is, is recall.

00:02:59.489 --> 00:03:04.098
It works as the ceiling for the whole system: if the correct answer doesn't reach

00:03:04.098 --> 00:03:08.025
the context, the model has no way of choosing it, however good it is.

00:03:08.325 --> 00:03:12.971
It's a deterministic, cheap, fast test, and it tells you whether the problem lies

00:03:12.971 --> 00:03:14.864
before the model or in the model.

00:03:16.164 --> 00:03:17.176
Step 4.

00:03:17.476 --> 00:03:19.662
Compare methods and K values.

00:03:20.212 --> 00:03:24.893
Compare methods (lexical versus embeddings) and K values on that same recall.

00:03:25.193 --> 00:03:27.065
Don't assume embeddings win.

00:03:27.615 --> 00:03:32.193
With recall as the metric you can compare options fairly: lexical method against

00:03:32.193 --> 00:03:34.711
embeddings, and different K values for each.

00:03:35.011 --> 00:03:36.105
Build a table.

00:03:36.405 --> 00:03:38.277
Don't assume embeddings win.

00:03:38.577 --> 00:03:43.163
In tasks with proper names, codes or abbreviations, lexical similarity often

00:03:43.163 --> 00:03:45.697
performs as well or better, and it's free.

00:03:45.997 --> 00:03:49.796
Embeddings help when the relationship is one of meaning rather than spelling.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:51.933
Let your data decide.

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:54.362
Step 5.

00:03:54.662 --> 00:03:56.568
The full eval with each K.

00:03:57.118 --> 00:04:01.301
Run the full eval, 5 times, with the full list and with each K.

00:04:01.601 --> 00:04:05.423
Compare accuracy, costly errors, tokens and cost.

00:04:05.973 --> 00:04:09.412
Recall measures the prefilter, not the final result.

00:04:09.712 --> 00:04:14.556
Run the eval end to end, five times, with the full list and with each K value.

00:04:14.856 --> 00:04:18.678
Compare accuracy, costly errors, tokens and cost.

00:04:18.978 --> 00:04:23.616
You'll see something that tends to surprise: more context doesn't always give a

00:04:23.616 --> 00:04:24.438
better result.

00:04:24.738 --> 00:04:29.059
With many similar candidates in the prompt, the model gets confused more.

00:04:29.359 --> 00:04:33.905
There's a point beyond which adding candidates worsens quality and raises cost at

00:04:33.905 --> 00:04:34.691
the same time.

00:04:35.991 --> 00:04:37.178
Step 6.

00:04:37.478 --> 00:04:39.930
Choose the smallest K that suffices.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.942
Pick the smallest K that keeps recall and accuracy.

00:04:44.242 --> 00:04:48.309
More similar candidates can confuse the model and make results worse.

00:04:48.859 --> 00:04:53.621
The decision rule is choosing the smallest K that keeps recall and accuracy within

00:04:53.621 --> 00:04:54.724
the baseline range.

00:04:55.024 --> 00:04:57.860
Document the full table and the chosen value.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.525
Add prefilter recall as a permanent test in CI: if someone changes the method or the

00:05:03.525 --> 00:05:06.847
catalog grows a lot, recall can drop without notice.

00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:11.903
That test takes seconds, and it protects a piece that, when it fails, makes

00:05:11.903 --> 00:05:13.488
everything after it fail.

00:05:14.788 --> 00:05:15.894
An example.

00:05:16.444 --> 00:05:21.330
With illustrative numbers: a system sends a catalog of two thousand records on every

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.656
call, about sixty thousand input tokens.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:28.673
The team builds a trigram prefilter and measures recall on eighty labeled cases.

00:05:28.973 --> 00:05:35.807
With K equal to five, the correct answer is present in 90% of cases; with ten, in

00:05:35.807 --> 00:05:39.773
97%; with twenty, in 99%; with fifty, also 99%.

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:44.963
They try embeddings and with K equal to twenty get 98%: no improvement.

00:05:45.263 --> 00:05:50.062
In the full eval, K equal to twenty gives the same accuracy as the full list, with

00:05:50.062 --> 00:05:54.159
one fewer costly error per run and around three percent of the tokens.

00:05:54.459 --> 00:05:57.677
With fifty, accuracy drops two points.

00:05:58.977 --> 00:06:00.640
The most common mistakes.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:04.409
Shrinking context without measuring prefilter recall.

00:06:04.959 --> 00:06:07.504
Assuming embeddings beat a lexical method.

00:06:08.054 --> 00:06:11.366
Evaluating only cost and not end-to-end quality.

00:06:11.916 --> 00:06:14.055
Choosing a large K "to be safe".

00:06:15.355 --> 00:06:17.598
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:18.148 --> 00:06:21.227
You have baseline input tokens and quality.

00:06:21.777 --> 00:06:24.996
Prefilter recall is measured for several K values.

00:06:25.546 --> 00:06:28.683
The full eval ran with the full list and with each K.

00:06:29.233 --> 00:06:32.173
Prefilter recall runs as a test in CI.

00:06:33.473 --> 00:06:35.368
And where AI fell short.

00:06:35.918 --> 00:06:40.507
With many similar candidates in context, the model picks wrong more often.

00:06:40.807 --> 00:06:43.991
Context is designed and measured, not piled up.

00:06:45.291 --> 00:06:46.872
That was day 19.

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:52.146
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:52.146 --> 00:06:53.154
asked questions.

00:06:53.704 --> 00:06:59.182
Continue with day 20: Prompt regression: version, compare and don't break what

00:06:59.182 --> 00:06:59.674
worked.
